WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.853
Welcome to Grade 7 English, Unit
8: Media, Technology, and News.

2
00:00:03.933 --> 00:00:06.250
This unit is called "Media, Technology,

3
00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:13.440
and News" because you will explore how information travels
in the modern world and why it matters to think critically

4
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.038
about everything you read,

5
00:00:15.118 --> 00:00:16.083
watch, and share.

6
00:00:16.163 --> 00:00:23.150
Every day, millions of messages flow through newspapers,
television channels, websites, and social media platforms.

7
00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.899
Some of these messages are carefully researched and truthful.

8
00:00:26.979 --> 00:00:30.340
Others are misleading, exaggerated, or completely false.

9
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.290
As a young person growing up in the digital age,

10
00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:40.603
one of the most important skills you can develop is the
ability to tell the difference between reliable information and

11
00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:41.525
misinformation.

12
00:00:41.605 --> 00:00:46.687
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

13
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:48.431
By the end of this unit,

14
00:00:48.511 --> 00:00:55.770
you will be able to explain what media literacy
means and why it is important for young people today.

15
00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:02.164
You will also identify the key features of
a reliable news source and an unreliable one.

16
00:01:02.244 --> 00:01:09.720
You will also read a text about media and answer
comprehension and analysis questions in full sentences.

17
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.409
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

18
00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:18.489
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: express,
claim, allege, correspond, explanation, influence.

19
00:01:18.569 --> 00:01:23.180
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

20
00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.829
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

21
00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:33.110
The reading sequence includes Media Literacy: Reading
the News with Care and Breaking News at Kilima Academy.

22
00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:33.916
Read actively.

23
00:01:33.996 --> 00:01:37.893
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

24
00:01:37.973 --> 00:01:41.638
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

25
00:01:41.718 --> 00:01:46.125
The first language focus is Present
Perfect + Just, and Reported Speech.

26
00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:52.794
The present perfect with just announces that something
happened moments ago, which is why news reports say,

27
00:01:52.874 --> 00:01:55.848
"The government has just announced a new policy."

28
00:01:55.928 --> 00:02:01.582
Reported speech then lets a journalist pass on
what someone said without quoting every word.

29
00:02:01.662 --> 00:02:05.633
Together, these two patterns are
the engine room of news English.

30
00:02:05.713 --> 00:02:12.426
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

31
00:02:12.506 --> 00:02:16.814
In speaking, you will complete Speaking
1: Debate: Digital Media Ethics.

32
00:02:16.894 --> 00:02:20.898
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

33
00:02:20.978 --> 00:02:24.006
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

34
00:02:24.086 --> 00:02:27.236
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

35
00:02:27.316 --> 00:02:28.767
You are ready to begin.

36
00:02:28.847 --> 00:02:35.358
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

37
00:02:35.438 --> 00:02:38.553
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

38
00:02:38.633 --> 00:02:45.943
Use AI English for hints and feedback any time, and bring
your questions to a live session too if you join one.
